  "dom_id": "step-q15663",
  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/28ab65439d4ee144f9928e3b5048045858d4207794f886622b0961893d98615a.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{(g(2))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(2)=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $g(2)=-1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $0.$ So, ${g}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{(g(2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-1)-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 0}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
